package typeinfo

// PreserveAdaptiveEncoding returns the fresh TypeInfo with its storage encoding pinned to the
// existing TypeInfo's encoding, when both represent the same type family that uses variable
// adaptive encoding.
//
// A freshly-constructed TypeInfo (e.g. one built from a SQL type during an ALTER TABLE) reports
// its encoding based on the global UseAdaptiveEncoding flag. That means a column originally
// persisted under a legacy address encoding (e.g. val.StringAddrEnc) would silently adopt the
// global adaptive-encoding default if the flag flipped to true between the time the table was
// created and the time it is modified. Persisting the fresh encoding on top of data written with
// the legacy encoding corrupts reads. This function prevents that by carrying the existing,
// already-persisted encoding onto the fresh TypeInfo.
//
// It is a no-op (returns fresh unchanged) when:
//   - existing is nil (nothing to preserve),
//   - the two TypeInfos belong to different type families (e.g. VARCHAR->TEXT, Point->LineString),
//   - or the type family does not use variable adaptive encoding (e.g. integer types).
//
// It returns nil when fresh is nil.
func PreserveAdaptiveEncoding(existing TypeInfo, fresh TypeInfo) TypeInfo {
	if fresh == nil {
		return nil
	}
	if existing == nil {
		return fresh
	}

	// Only the type families below use a variable encoding that depends on the global
	// UseAdaptiveEncoding flag. For every other family the encoding is fixed by the concrete
